THE PASCALINE DESIGN RATIONALE

S. A. Moore

05/2007

Introduction
------------

One of the better documents to come from the Ada project was the the 
"rationale", which introduced the designers and the reason for the decisions
for the language. A rationale both gives a place for the designer to state the
reasons for the language features, and also serves to keep such opinonated 
verbage out of the language standard itself.

The Pascaline rationale follows in those footsteps. The format of the rationale
for the most part follows the Pascaline standard itself, and can be read to
follow along with this document.

Why extend Pascal?
------------------

This is really the hardest question of all. I have been both a Pascal user and
Pascal compiler designer for nearly 30 years now. When I first implemented 
Pascal in 1979 (the compiler first operated in January, 1980), I had both the
original Pascal documents as well as the draft standard for what would become
ISO 7185 (note that this was four years before the introduction of 
"Turbo Pascal"). The language design was mature at that time, and I implemented
a compiler that was strict to the standard.

My first implementation of the language added several features I felt necessary
at that time to make the language usable on a microprocessor, including separate
compilation, ability to include other source files inside the current source
file, the ability to declare external assembly language procedures and functions
(as was allowed for, but not fully specified by the standard), ability to
functions, procedures and even variables at fixed addresses, ability to specify
alternate radixes, character escapes, etc.

In fact, the 1980 implementation of Pascal looked very much like a cross between
Pascal and C. This was not a coincidence, as these were the two main programming
languages I used throughout the 1980's, and on through today.

I felt in 1980 that all of the extra features I needed to make Pascal practical
for everyday use then could be covered by the label of "implementation specific
features".

However, as my day to day use of the language went on, I felt the distinct need
the support of features above and beyond Pascal that were formal parts of the
language. The need to always use fixed length arrays topped the list of 
problems. The next was a desire to have a formal solution to modularity. Then
there was the need to exactly specify the precision of operations (although 
Pascal specifies the exact value variables can hold, it specifies that all 
subranges of integer are promoted to full integer during expressions).

Why not use another language?
-----------------------------

This question is much easier. Despite the introduction of several newer 
languages that did cover my needs with Pascal, many of those candidates do not
share the essential features of Pascal, such as type safety, simplicity, 
clarity, and others. Ada is certainly type safe, but nobody would claim that it
is simple. C and C++ discard type safety entirely, and also a good deal of
clarity. Java and C# reintroduce type safety, but not completely, and feature
(quite unfortunately in my opinion) the obscurity of odd characters instead of
easy to read keywords, endless and unecessary operators, arcane syntax, etc.

In fact, the closest languages that are candiates for safety, simplicity and
clarity are Nicklaus Wirths' other languages such as Modula and Oberon. This
leads to my next point.

I don't really see the point of discarding your language each time you see the
idea of something better on the horizon. Computer language research is 
important, and new languages should be introduced to cover new concepts. In the
idea laboratories of the colleges and research laboratories of the world. In the
world of day to day work, we in the trenches have the right to ask:

"that is a great new language feature. Can you add that to my current language?"

And indeed, no matter what language you prefer, this has indeed been the rule.
Thus we have C going to C++, continuing new versions of Ada, Java, and even
Fortran and Cobol. In these languages, the answer has been to incorporate the
new with the old, and even to make the new and the old work together and 
interlock seamlessly, which may indeed be a more difficult task that simply
writing a new language from scratch.

Why not use one of the existing extentions for Pascal?
------------------------------------------------------

This is perhaps back to being a more difficult question. The short answer is
there is really only one such candidate, the ISO 10206 specification from 1990.
There are others, such as the UCSD dielect and the Borland Dielect. However,
both of these were never compliant with the original Pascal language. Certainly
it is possible to add back the missing constructs from ISO 7185 Pascal to such
a language, but often the two languages are in conflict, such that an "option"
is required to switch between interpretations of the language.

I feel strongly that such an amalgamated language inherently disappoints its
potiential users. The existing users of the dielect will think of such a new
langugage as an odd implementation of their language and avoid its use. The
standard language users will find that they need to break the standard to use
the dielect extentions.

Further, it is difficult to organically extend a language that hasn't needed to
comply with the original language. Extending ISO 7185 Pascal involves a lot of
work and compromise to make the new features work with the old. The designers of
the dielect never felt that need because they never implemented the original
language. To enter into this domain is to shackle oneself with chains others
cannot see or appreciate.

This leaves to basic extentions to Pascal that have appeared, that are compliant
in full with original Pascal. These are the ISO 10206 standard and Pascal-XSC.

The first issue with ISO 10206 Pascal is that it does not implement many of the
extention goals I had for the language. This is not a problem in and of itself,
for the standard can be a starting point for such further extentions.

The second issue is that the standard starts to distinctly erode the ideals of
simplicity and clarity of original Pascal. There are no less than four (!)
different methods available to operate on arrays of characters, the original
Pascal method using fixed arrays of characters, conformat array parameters,
a new string type, and finally schemas.

Finally, there are distinct areas where the ISO 10206 standard requires less
efficient implementation. A prime example is the requirement to pass both the
high and the low limit of variable length arrays.

For Pascal-XSC, a standard I admire and refer to, there is simply not a great
deal of commonality in the goals of it (primarily extended mathematic operators)
and mine. Add to this the fact that Pascal-XSC exists in one implementation
only, and has a limited interest group.

The language Pascaline
----------------------

If, as Nicklaus Wirth describes, Pascal is a "medium" complexity language, then
Pascaline certainly qualifies as a "high" complexity language. However, the bar
has moved with the specifications of such languages as Ada and C++.

Pascaline is a relatively simple improvement over Pascal. The text of the main
extentions is about 10-14 pages, verses the 54 pages of the original "Pascal
Report" by Nicklaus Wirth in 1973. This is not counting the suggested standard
libraries and methods added as appendicies to the Pascaline standard.

Keywords
--------

The first extention Pascaline (or any other Pascal extention) is going to 
feature is a set of added keywords, 24 to be exact.

It has been an opinion that no extention to Pascal should be presented with a
new keyword at all. This would mean that instead of defining a new keyword, you
would define it as an identifier with a "special meaning". The ISO 7185 standard
strongly hints at this with the classification of "forward" and "external" as
"directives" and not keywords.

While noting that the ISO 10206 standard apparently has no issue with 
introducing new keywords to the language (leading perhaps to the idea that
addition of keywords is a special priveledge for standards comitees only), it
seems to me that this goes back to the basic issue of "what is a keyword".

There have, in fact, been several languages that haven't defined keywords at
all (or more properly "reserved words"), leading to the ability of the user to
use what would appear to be keywords as identifiers. This leads to fun looking
constructs such as:

for for := to to do do <statement>;

which would be perfectly valid if "for", "to" and "do" could be identifiers. We
don't do this in modern languages because, besides being confusing for the
programmer/program reader, it also increases the difficulty for the compiler to
recover from errors, since it has no distinct tolkens to look to in order to
resyncronise the parse after such an error.

Based on this, Pascaline marches forward not only with new, distinct keywords
where necessary, but also changes the definition of "forward" and "external" to
official keywords. In ISO 1785 mode, a Pascaline compiler does not define them
as keywords, but in Pascaline mode it certainly does.

With the definition of new keywords, there is a price. There is always a 
possibility that an existing program will use such a keyword as an identifier in
an ISO 7185 program. This is the first and only modal dependency of Pascaline.
That is to say, taking an existing ISO 1785 program and compiling it as a 
Pascaline program, this is the only thing that would cause it to not compile.

Identifiers
-----------

With identifiers, we enter into a long list of common extentions that everyone
implements in one form or another. The standard "break" or "separator" method
used in the literature for Pascal was something like:

MyNewVariable

or capitalization of each word in a composite identifier. Pascaline adds the "_"
character as an official label character for use as a break. It is 
"significant". This means that:

my_new_variable

and

mynewvariable

are NOT the same identifier.

The only remaining issue of interest here is that there is a convention, 
apparently starting with C, that identifiers starting with "_", like:

_dangerous_system_symbol

Have special meanings. This is apparently for the idea that when writing 
assembly language support functions, it is best to choose a name that won't
conflict with an identifer in the compiled program.

Now I personally have never felt the urge to use "_" prefixed system identifers.
The Pascaline implementation uses a "coining" method to translate program 
identifers to identifiers acceptable to the assembly/link layer such that for
the program "test", you would have identifiers like:

test_a
test_mynewvariable

etc. This system inherently avoids name collisions with common assembly support
code, while still allowing the creation of assembly code to access program
identifiers.

Never the less, Pascaline recommends that "_" prefixed identifiers be avoided,
and recommends an option to reject them.

Numeric constants
-----------------

Pascaline features single character prefixes for alternate radixes, with "$"
for hexadecimal, "&" for octal, and "%" for binary.

Despite resemblances to other Pascals, the convention (which by the way predates
may other Pascals including Turbo Pascal) was stolen blatantly from the 
conventions used in Motorolas' assemblers. The "@" used by motorola for octal
was changed to "&" in Pascaline because the ISO 7185 standard appropriated it
for use with systems that (apparently) didn't have a "^" character. So here we
have a perfectly good convention that was ruined by machines and terminals that
most certainly no longer exist.

There are several basic ways to implement alternate radixes, and I'll list some
of them here:

$1234   - Single character prefix
16#1234 - Radix prefix
0x1234  - C and many other languages
1234h   - Postfix, copied from assembler notation

There are two reasons I prefer the single character prefix. First, it is simple,
both to parse, and to write. Second, it tells the compiler what radix is being
used when the number starts. The use of "1234h" postfix formats requires that
the number have its characters stored and reread when the actual radix is found
at the end. I don't find this format amusing even for assemblers, and I have
never written an assembler that way.

The C standard "0x1234" format is borrowed from many compilers and assemblers
that existed before C did (I have seen the convention used back into the 1960s).
The idea is that it will be recognised as a simple decimal, and will have led
the compiler completely into parsing a decimal "0" when the parser can notice
that it is followed by a postfix "x", and then switch to parsing hex. The
convention is fairly inexplicable and uses two characters to do what a single
character can, but perhaps having the advantage of not using up a precious
special character that the language might use. Important to C, but not to 
Pascal.

The prefix convention "16#1234", used in many languages, including ISO 10206
Pascal, is a good one for being able to represent all radixes. It is, of course,
verbose. My chief issue with it is that, in my entire carreer, I have never been
asked to use base 32 in a program. Certainly at some point, base 32 (or 
greater!) will in fact come into use, but I will certainly be breath and warmth
challenged by that point in time.

A final mention is about the binary ("%") convention in Pascaline. This is not
often useful, but when it is, such as the creation of a bit mask in an embedded
product, it is greatly appreciated.

Constant expressions
--------------------

The disadvantage of original Pascals' scheme of introducing the declarations in
sucessive levels, i.e., constants to types to variables and procedure/functions,
is that it discards the menmonic power of expressions. I suppose the point was
that such calculations were more clearly left to the code blocks, but the effect
was to cause programmers to perform such setup calculations with a pocket
calculator, then place them in the program, perhaps placing the founding
calculation in a comment to preserve the original idea.

For such constants, there is almost universal agreement amongst Pascal 
implementors that such constant expressions should not simply be standard
expressions from the language, but rather a special set of rules applying to
constants only. This avoids the idea of what to do if the programmer places a
variable, function or worse in such an expression.

Boolean integer operations
--------------------------

I wonder if anyone noticed in original Pascal that the language was not 
dependent on if it was implemented on a binary or a decimal machine. D. E. Knuth
would have been proud of such a development, as in his famous series "the art of
programming" he described the use of assembly language that would not care what
the base of numbers in the machine were.

To appreciate why this is interesting, you have to appreciate that as late as
the 1960's, there were still two kinds of computers in use, the decimal based
ones and the binary based ones. Even if you considered binary to be the 
"winner", there were machines that supported both types of math, including the
famous IBM 360.

So in Pascaline, there is support for direct binary math on integers. The
standard does not get into the issue of what that means on a signed integer. 
I think this is just, for the reason that Pascaline also supports the idea of
unsigned types, and therefore removes any great need to perform binary math with
signed integers. If you want to perform binary operations on integers, then it
is only fair to use only unsigned types to do it.

The standard specifies that these operations probally won't work on a decimal
machine. Don't laugh, there is still a good possibility that the language may be
used on computer history simulators.

Protected parameters
--------------------

There isn't much controversy here. Everyone implements such parameters. In fact,
an early version of Wirths' Pascal report had them. The import of a protected
parameter is that any expression that can be allowed with a value parameter can
be used, but the compiler is able to eliminate the copy operation involved with
passing structured types into a value parameter.

The only issue is the choice of keyword, "view", vs. "protected" or anything 
else. My only excuse is that "view" seemed to me to be more in keeping with the
series of a value, variable or ... parameter. In other words, "I pass by value",
"I pass by variable", then "I pass by view" made more sense to me than "I pass
by protected". It just rhymed better. I have the value for the parameter, I have
the variable, I have a view on the parameter. Plus "view" is a nice short
keyword.

Case statement extentions
-------------------------

Of all of the extentions in Pascaline, I find the case statement extentions most
distasteful. The problem with "else" (or "otherwise") and case ranges is that
they really remove the best property of case statements, that of being a clear
simple to understand construct that compiles efficiently to an underlying
machine level construct, namely a table lookup jump.

The extentions encourage, and I have often seen in code examples, the idea of a
case statement as a "general purpose" filter for values. It certainly isn't. If
there are a lot of gaps between the sequences of case labels, the compiler isn't
going to give you a neat jump table in the output code, but is going to be
forced to recode the problem in another way. And that "way" may not have the
performance you expect from a case statement. In fact, the compiler can just
degenerate the case statement to a series of if-then-else comparison statements
internally.

Of course, this can happen with ISO 7185 Pascal as well:

case x of

   1: ...;
   1000000: ...;

end;

Is a fairly ridiculous case statement, but perfectly valid under the rules of
ISO 7185 Pascal. The result is sure to be something you won't like, such as a
case jump table of massive length, but only two entries occupied, degeneration
to if-then-else, etc.

The issue people seem to have with ISO 7185 Pascals standard case statement is
that it "takes too much code" to get around the fact that the default for a case
statement is an error:

if (x < 1) or (x > 3) then ....
else case x of

   1: ...;
   2: ...;
   3: ...;

end;

I must admit that I don't share the idea of single if statement to guard a case
statement as a hardship. A bit more difficult is collecting all of the gaps in a
series into an if conditional on a large but slightly sparse case table.

In any case, the "straw that broke the camels' back" was using Pascaline to 
interface to C code, which is pretty much a requirement these days. Trying to
efficiently dispatch a deliberately sparse enum statement from a C code module
using a Pascal case statement is an excersize in frustration, so I gave up and
gave in to the "dark side".

The use of the "else" keyword (vs. "otherwise") was based on else being a short
keyword that already existed in the language and already had the intended 
meaning ("else do this"). It didn't hurt that Turbo Pascal also had this 
convention.

The idea of ranges was introduced in the CDC 6000 series Pascal compiler, and
used by Nicklaus Wirth himself in Oberon. It seems reasonable, and solves the
issue where you are having to count off enumerations redundantly.












